  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 AM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery with 500mb heights plotted shows broad upper \nridging to nearly zonal flow prevailing over the middle of the \ncountry. In the middle and lower levels, high pressure is centered \nover the Ohio Valley into the Southeast. This is leading to easterly \nto southeasterly flow at the surface and southerly to southwesterly \nflow in the mid-levels. This flow regime is leading to modest \nmoisture advection into eastern Kansas and western Missouri. \nIsentropic ascent, noted from 295K to 300K, was leading to scattered \nto widespread showers and storms from northeastern Kansas into \nnorthwestern Missouri, with decreasing coverage from northern into \nnortheastern Missouri. Forecast soundings across northern Missouri \nshow steep lapse rates above an inversion with a nearly saturated \nprofile below that inversion. This leads to fairly robust elevated \ninstability, for late October, with 1000-1500 J/kg of elevated \ninstability. With the strongest mid and upper-level winds pushed \nnorth of the region, shear is relatively weak, and that is the \nmain limiting factor preventing stronger convection across \nnortheastern Kansas through northern Missouri. That said, with \nthe ascent noted from 295K to 300K, instability available, and \nahead of shortwave trough moving northeastward out of the \nSouthern Plains, we should see scattered to widespread shower \nand thunderstorm activity today for areas along and north of the\nMissouri river. \n\nFor Saturday, ascent looks weaker/less focused, leading to lower \nchances for precipitation overall, despite what looks like thick \ncloud cover. Coverage of precipitation looks more isolated to \nscattered in this pattern. Transient shortwave ridging builds into \nthe area Sunday, limiting precipitation chances. That shortwave \nridging is in response to a deepening trough moving east from the \nRockies. Strong southerly/southwesterly low to mid-level flow \ndevelops leading to very strong moisture transport into the region. \nThis leads to very anomalously high precipitable water values\nthat are near or at the max percentiles in the NAEFS \nclimatology. Instability looks weak overall but given the strong\nkinematics, we'll need to watch for the potential for \nstrong/severe storms in what may be a high-shear, low-CAPE \nenvironment across eastern Kansas and western Missouri for \nMonday afternoon and evening. \n\nIn the wake of this early week system, temperatures fall back to \nnear-normal levels, in the lower 60s for much of the area, for \nmiddle to later part of next week. The shortwave associated with the \nearly week precipitation chances may be slow to depart the area. \nDeterministic models show the upper-low getting cutoff and \nmeandering through the region. There's some indication of this in \nthe ensemble 500mb clusters, though they never show the system \ncutting off. Rather, just slow eastward eastward progression and \nanomalously low 500mb heights through the lower Mississippi\nValley.",
